From 2babb63f579e8cb54c15c6b00a4456c777f88cec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:06:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Far characters: #pragma farchars, #pragma strings

#pragma farchars(n) makes far space n the character space: Declaration::FarSpace
answers it for a one-byte integer type, so a byte reached through a pointer is
translated through farxlN/farxoN/farxzN like a typed space's, and the program's
routines tell by the address (one below the space's range comes back as it is).
Struct fields keep their struct's space, locals and near globals stay near, a
volatile byte is hardware's, and functions defined under #pragma farchars(off)
(COPT_FAR_CHARS_OFF) use plain addresses. An object of a section in the
character space keeps to a page (LOBJF_NEVER_CROSS), so a built-in strcpy,
memcpy or memset within one is translated once, as for a far struct; with a
character space, MapLateIntrinsics leaves memcpy() calls alone. A pointer leaving
the character space gives no warning 2026 (its address still tells).

#pragma strings(codesection, stringsection) sends the string literals written
while codesection is the code section to stringsection.

With -dOSCAR_FARCHARS, string.c's assembler strcpy, strcmp, memcpy and memset
translate each pointer once at entry through the program's farxzc (declared in
string.h as a C function, as crt.c declares main), so they also translate plain
addresses passed from farchars(off) code: hardware code copies with its own
loops.

autotest/farcharstest.c: literals in the space, tables of them, a writable buffer
there, near buffers through pointers, struct fields untranslated (counted), void *
round trips, the built-ins, the library routines, page keeping over 24 literals,
volatile and farchars(off) plain; -n at -O0 to -O3 with farxl6/farxo6, farxz6,
and the library: 15 of 15. With the space mapped to itself the test never ends.
make check: exit 0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
